(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608319400-oak-the-ontology-as-the-admission-boundary")
  (:adr/title "OaK: the ontology as the admission boundary for an agent's calls")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607201300-kotoba-safe-application-language"
                "adr-2607279200-kotoba-centered-migration-plan"
                "adr-2608261100-kotoba-app-vertical-slice-migration-unit"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

The owner pointed at OaK -- Ontology-as-a-Kernel, from `Toward Effective and
Reliable LLM Agents via Dynamic Ontology` (Zhang, Sun, Yang, Cui, Guo, Hu;
arXiv:2608.22974) -- and asked for it to be implemented on amu and Kotoba.

**The paper was not read.** The specification implemented is the description
the owner supplied: the kernel is `K = (S, F)`, a task-oriented ontology
schema plus the executable reasoning functions permitted over it, and the
agent computes through `F` rather than over the store. Treat the
correspondence as a reading of the idea, not as fidelity to its algorithms.
The dynamic half -- building `S` and `F` from training data and refining them
against a judge -- is NOT implemented; `S` is hand-written and fixed.

## Decision

`kotoba-lang/oak`. One `.kotoba` guest, one exported entry (`oak-call`), the
graph behind `:dataspace/transact`.

The framing lands on this stack almost without translation, because Kotoba
already refuses ambient authority. Three properties OaK argues for are not
things this implementation adds; they are things it cannot avoid:

- the agent computes only through `F` -- `F` is not exported, `oak-call` is
- the agent cannot reach the store -- the graph is behind capability id 24,
  and a guest that does not hold it cannot name a pattern
- a refusal is a result, not a crash -- no ambient `throw`, so every refusal
  is `[:rejected code detail]`

`S` is an ordinary document, so it has a `document-sha256` identity, and every
result carries it. A result is attributable to the schema that admitted it.
An entity's TYPE is not a field of the schema and not an argument of the call
-- it is an assertion in the graph, put there by an earlier admitted
`:declare`, so checking a domain constraint is itself a graph query.

`dataspace-v1` was chosen for the graph plane because it is the only
capability kit qualified on `:reference`, `:wasm-aot`, `:native-aot` AND
`:jit` (measured 2026-08-31; read the kit file, not this sentence).

## Two ceilings, and only one of them is the kernel's

A query result is one document and a document container holds
`document-container-item-limit` (32) items, so a 33rd fact would be one the
kernel could never report. The kernel refuses it on the WRITE side with
`:oak/graph-at-capacity`. It has to be the write side: the provider does not
answer 'too many', it traps while encoding its reply, and a Kotoba guest has
no `catch`.

Reading is bounded AGAIN by the host's fuel, and that ceiling is not ours.
Measured 2026-08-31 on the reference interpreter at default fuel: a census
answers at 24 declared entities and traps `:fuel-exhausted` at 28 -- so on
that host fuel runs out before the document does. The conformance suite
therefore does not name a size. It probes for the running host's own ceiling
and asserts the invariant on both sides: below it a read answers; above it
what comes out is a Kotoba trap naming `:fuel-exhausted`, never a wrong answer
and never a host error object. The same self-calibrating shape
`kir_host_stack_trap_test` uses, and for the same reason.

## Two upstream defects this uncovered, both fixed and merged

Neither was reachable from a `.clj` test, and the fleet gate for kotoba-kir is
`:jvm-test`, so nothing had executed the `:cljs` branches.

**1. Every indexed document operation was broken on ClojureScript.** An i64 is
a Long on the JVM and a BigInt on cljs, and `nth` / `assoc` / `subvec` / `inc`
all reject a BigInt. Each operation handed the host collection the i64 it had
just range-checked, so:

    (document-vector-at [10 20 30] 0)   ;; host TypeError escaped
    (document-vector-at [10 20 30] 9)   ;; clean none

The IN-RANGE read -- the one every real program does -- was the one that
threw, and the out-of-range read answered cleanly. **The broken path was the
quieter of the two**, and a test that only checked that a bad index yields
`none` would have stayed green while no document could be read at all.
Measured: all six of `document-vector-at`, `document-list-at`,
`document-map-entry-at`, `document-vector-assoc`, `document-vector-drop`,
`document-vector-remove`. Fixed by `container-host-index`; the regression test
fails 10 assertions without it and its out-of-range control passes either way.

**2. `document-sha256` was JVM-only.** The W4 exit-gate identity for a logical
document reached `MessageDigest` on the JVM and `(throw ...)` on cljs, while
every other document operation was portable. The thrown message said the
operation `requires the JVM/Node host path` -- and nbb IS Node -- which is why
the gap read as a deliberate restriction rather than a missing branch. The
test vector is pinned to `shasum -a 256` over the canonical bytes, so neither
host's own digest is the oracle for the other.

**2b. The first version of that fix was itself a regression, and this is the
part worth keeping.** It routed through `multiformats.core/sha256` on the
reasoning that io-multiformats is already a direct dependency. On `:cljs` that
namespace requires the npm package `@noble/hashes/sha2.js`, so
`kotoba.kir.value` -- which every value-carrying module loads -- gained an npm
dependency. It was not seen because the reasoning was checked against the
io-multiformats CHECKOUT on hand, which predates that require.

**Being a direct dependency says nothing about what the RESOLVED version of it
pulls in**, and the resolved version is the one in the consumer's lock. Now
`sha2.core`, which is the portable implementation multiformats itself reached
for, one link closer, with no host packages at all. The digest is unchanged.

What caught it was amu's own `--jvm-free` resolver, which fails closed:
advancing amu's pin turned `amu check --jvm-free` into `Cannot find module
'@noble/hashes/sha2.js'` while the same command on the unadvanced pin passed.
That control is the check to run before landing a change to a T0 namespace,
and it was not run.

## The wasm path, measured

`amu check --jvm-free` passed on the kernel from the first day and
`amu compile --jvm-free --target wasm32-browser` did not: an i64 reached a
host operation that cannot take a BigInt in two more places -- `uleb` in
kotoba-kir and the capability import key in kotoba-wasm -- and the CLI reports
any host exception as an internal compiler error. Both are fixed, and
kotoba-wasm grew its own ClojureScript runner in the process, for the same
reason kotoba-kir did: every test in it was `.clj`.

Advancing amu's own pins to those fixes is what makes the documented CLI
command work; it is a pure fast-forward (kir +13, wasm +2) and goes through a
pull request, because amu's main is protected.

Measured after that, all JVM-free: the kernel compiles to an 8,394-byte
`wasm32-browser-kotoba-v1` module, instantiates on `runtime/browser-host.mjs`,
and returns the same `document-sha256` for S as the reference interpreter.

The parity check covers S's identity only, and it discriminates -- pointed at
a module built from a renamed entity type it fails with both digests printed.
A check asserting only `is a sha256` would have passed, and that is nearly
what it was: its first run compared against an interpreter that had thrown,
and reported a failure for the wrong reason.

**Admission itself now runs on the module too**, against a real dataspace --
the same `provider.dataspace` the interpreter run uses, with a bridge either
side of it. The bridge is small because the host's document form IS the KIR
document form; a keyword is the text :is-a wrapped in quotes on one side and the keyword
`:is-a` on the other.

Two things had to change, and both are measured properties of the host rather
than preferences.

**A call cannot arrive from outside.** `browser-host.mjs` admits a compound
argument only if the host itself minted it -- `assertValue` rejects anything
else as `forged compound typed value rejected` -- and the constructors it
exposes (`instance.typedValues`) are `vectorI64`, `vectorF64`, `stringIndex`,
`disjointSetI64`, `document`, `bytes`. No record, no variant. So the kernel
carries `call-declare` / `call-relate` / `call-related` / `call-census`, which
take scalars and assemble the call value inside the guest. The other direction
is open: a provider's result goes through `admitHostResult`, which mints from
plain frozen arrays.

That makes the earlier phrasing wrong. `oak-call` is no longer `the only
door`; there are five entries. **The property that was ever load-bearing is
that admission is reached in exactly one place**, and it still is -- each
entry assembles a call and hands it to `oak-call`. Conformance now asserts the
export set AND that no `do-*` / `all-*` / `type-of` is in it, so a future
export that reaches the dataspace unadmitted fails a check rather than
widening the surface quietly.

**One call spends one instance.** Fuel is spent over an instance's life, not
per call, so a run that reuses an instance traps `unreachable` partway through
-- measured after four checks. The dataspace outlives the instances because
the provider is the host's, which is the shape `amu/runtime/dom-driver.mjs`
already uses for the same reason.

Eleven checks pass on wasm. The suite discriminates on that backend as on the
other: the domain check made vacuous fails only the domain check, showing the
call falling through to the range check; query results discarded fails six.
The capacity and evidence mutations were run against the interpreter only --
the capacity one needs 34 instances on wasm, which is slow rather than hard.

## Evidence

19 conformance checks, run JVM-free under nbb (analyze -> lower -> instantiate
-> invoke, all `.cljc`). Green is cheap, so each was measured by breaking the
kernel one edit at a time and confirming that what went red was what the check
is named after:

- domain check made vacuous -> ONLY the domain check failed, showing the call
  falling through to the range check
- capacity guard removed -> the two capacity checks, reporting the provider's
  own `document-edn-read vector item limit exceeded`
- evidence digest replaced by a constant -> the digest check
- query results discarded -> six checks

That third mutation found a defect in the suite itself: the capacity block was
letting the provider's trap escape as an uncaught host error, so the run
STOPPED rather than reporting a failure. A check that cannot run must not exit
the way a check that ran and passed exits. Fixed before landing.

The runner exits 2 -- neither 0 nor 1 -- and names the missing repositories
when a sibling checkout is absent.

## Where this stands (2026-08-31)

Everything below is on a default branch. Nothing is waiting on a review.

| repo | commit | what |
|---|---|---|
| kotoba-lang/oak | 6f209ddf | the kernel, both suites, README |
| kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir | ff7a3ae2 | document indexing, document-sha256, uleb, the npm-free route |
| kotoba-lang/kotoba-wasm | 34557de9 | the capability import key, and the repo`s first cljs runner |
| kotoba-lang/amu | fb22f754 | the pin advance, via PR 715 |
| com-junkawasaki/root | this file, and the west entry for oak | |

Verification, from a fresh clone of the published pin, all JVM-free:

- `nbb bin/conformance.cljs` -- 20 checks, 0 failures, on the reference
  interpreter
- `nbb bin/wasm-conformance.cljs` -- 11 checks, 0 failures, on the compiled
  module against a real dataspace
- kotoba-kir`s own cljs suite -- 37 tests, 90 assertions, 0 failures
- kotoba-wasm`s new cljs suite -- 2 tests, 0 failures
- amu PR 715 -- both required checks green; `test (ubuntu-latest)` is 1,189
  tests / 8,727 assertions

## What is left, in the order it is worth doing

1. **The capacity and fuel ceilings are checked on the interpreter only.**
   Running them on wasm is slow rather than hard -- the capacity one needs 34
   instances, because fuel is spent per instance. Resume at
   `test/wasm-conformance.cljs`, whose `steps` helper already sequences one
   instance per call.
2. **The judge loop is not implemented and S is hand-written.** That is the
   dynamic half of the paper. Nothing here depends on it; a kernel is
   retargeted by editing `schema-doc`.
3. **A second consumer would justify moving the host bridge out of the test.**
   `host->kir-doc` / `kir->host-doc` / `kir->host-result` are general over the
   dataspace kit, not over oak, and probably belong beside
   `provider.dataspace` rather than in a suite. One consumer is not yet a
   library.
4. **The i64-is-a-BigInt class keeps recurring.** amu ADR-0286 is one
   instance, this ADR records two more, and a fifth landed the same day in
   kotoba-wasm from another session. The only detector that works is running
   the ClojureScript path; both repositories now have a runner, and widening
   what those runners cover is what shrinks the class.

## Consequences

- New UI/agent work that needs a governed graph has a worked example of the
  admission pattern that does not require reading the compiler.
- The two kotoba-kir fixes make the whole document plane usable under nbb,
  which is the JVM-free path Q9 requires. This was blocking more than oak.
- What is NOT here: no judge loop, no schema learned from data, no
  multi-schema kernel, no retraction. `S` is edited by hand and the kernel is
  recompiled.
- The naming plane is `subject` (bare name). `oak` is a metaphor name, so the
  README names itself in its first paragraph and distinguishes itself from
  OAK, the Ontology Access Kit, which is a different project.")))
